e is negative, implying an opposite raw contrast, and the upper bound of the signed interval is only about +0.018, below the approximate 10% threshold for the hypothesized stronger dependency. H33 and H17 are rejected because their signed intervals include at most small effects in the hypothesized direction, with point estimates near null or opposite. H36 is rejected in its registered direction because KRAS mutation in BRAF-WT cells produced a negative signed estimate, i.e., the raw contrast went in the opposite direction of the proposed weaker BRAF dependency; however the lower interval bound suggests a possible opposite pattern, so I proposed a reversed-BRAF follow-up hypothesis rather than accepting the registered claim. H30 and H21 are unresolved but deferred rather than rejected because, although their point estimates are small and interactions are not supported, their intervals reach moderately clinically plausible ranges, especially H30 where the MYC-amplified TP53-loss cell has only n=60. These are underpowered exploratory interaction checks and not high-priority validation targets. The overall pattern from this batch is that the strongest scientifically meaningful signals in this dataset are SMARCA4 loss with stronger SMARCA2 dependency, MSI-high with stronger WRN dependency, KEAP1 loss with weaker NFE2L2 dependency, and TP53 loss with weaker USP7 dependency, while amplification effects and p53-MYC/MYC-ribosome screens are mostly near null or context-dependent. Residual confounding by co-mutations, culture, batch, CRISPR library, screen quality, and omics completeness remains possible for all claims.",
